From 4bf64fd1e3a593c1047e37935bc0e936db7579cb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 01:53:19 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] windowing/gbm: follow a display that is connected after Kodi
 has started

Kodi chooses its output when it starts and stays on it. A TV that is
plugged in or turned on afterwards was not there to be chosen: Kodi
stayed on the panel of the laptop, or on no display at all, until it
was started again by hand. And a laptop that showed Kodi on its own
panel because no TV was seen said nothing of it: the one place where a
display can be chosen, the Monitor setting, simply did not offer one.

CDRMDisplayWatch listens for the hot-plug events of the graphics
devices (udev) and, after one, looks at the outputs of all of them the
way the start does (CDRMUtils::LookAtOutputs(), the code of the start
without its second look). When a display has appeared that the start
would have chosen over the output in use, the user is asked, with a
question that answers itself after 20 s, and Kodi is started again,
which is how it takes another output. Where Kodi shows itself on no
output there is nobody to ask, and it is started again at once.

What counts is decided in DRMOutputChoice.h, with nothing of DRM in
it, and tested on the host:
- only a display that was not there at the last look. One that was
  there when Kodi started and was not chosen is never a reason to move,
  so the start that follows cannot lead back to the question;
- only an output that ranks above the one in use, by the ranks of the
  start. One of the same rank is no reason, whichever of the two the
  order of the devices would give;
- never the output in use itself, whatever happens to its display;
- not an output for which the user said to stay, until Kodi is started
  again.

Kept out of the way of playback and of the machines that need none of
it:
- nothing is looked at while a video plays, nor in the 10 s after one
  was seen to play (an output that is asked reads its display again,
  which a driver may hold a frame up for; the end of Dolby Vision sets
  the output up again);
- nothing is looked at where no other output could rank above the one
  in use: a TV that takes Dolby Vision, or the output named in the
  settings. On such a machine this is a thread that waits on a socket;
- the outputs at the start are those that the start itself saw: no
  device is opened once more for them;
- what only the thread that draws may ask of Kodi (whether the
  interface is up, whether a video plays, what the settings name) is
  asked of it: the watch sets a flag and the next frame answers
  (CWinSystemGbm::FollowDisplays(), two flags read per frame);
- the question is Kodi's own yes/no dialog, and the application thread
  puts it by itself: the watch posts it and looks for the answer now
  and then. Neither waits for the other (a thread that waits for a
  dialog cannot be stopped until the dialog closes, and Kodi can be
  told to stop from within one). An answer that no person could have
  given (at once, the dialog being in use) is no answer, and nothing
  but the answer that moves, moves anything.

It also tells, once after each start, when Kodi is on the panel of the
machine itself and nothing is connected to any output for a TV, with
the names of those outputs: the first thing to know when the TV stays
dark, and what the Monitor setting could not say. Naming the panel in
that setting turns the notice off.

Not followed: a display in use that goes away (a TV that is turned off
looks the same, and Kodi must stay), and a graphics device that is
added or removed while Kodi runs.

Seen on an ASUS ROG Strix G513QY, where the kernel reported nothing on
the HDMI socket with the TV on: Kodi came up on the panel and offered
no other monitor. This does not make that socket work; it says what
Kodi sees, and moves to the TV once the kernel reports one.

Not run on a graphics device: the decision is tested on the host, the
rest is read and compiled.
